{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—too rhythmic, too clean—and the sun hung motionless at precisely 7:14 a.m., casting the same golden streak across his bedroom wall every morning for the last seven days. He rubbed his eyes, expecting sleep fog, but the pixels sharpened under his fingers like a screen being recalibrated. His coffee mug, half-full, displayed a tiny HUD icon: “Beverage: 87% consumed.” He screamed, and the game responded with a gentle chime: “Player distress detected. Would you like to restart level?” He stumbled to the mirror and saw his reflection glitch—his face flickered between his own and a low-poly avatar with a default smile. The walls hummed faintly, and when he pressed his palm against them, a translucent menu slid into view: “Objective: Live. Duration: Indefinite. Failure: Not an option.”\n\n***\n\nHe walked outside, and the neighborhood was a perfect replica of his old street—but every neighbor waved at exactly the same time, every dog barked in synchronized thirds, and the mailman delivered the same letter on the same porch, day after day. He found a hidden terminal behind the garage, its screen glowing with code that whispered his name. “You were chosen,” it read. “Not because you were special, but because you were forgettable.” He thought of his quiet life, his unremarkable routines, his lack of regrets. Maybe that was the point. He sat on the curb and waited. The sky blinked once—then reset. He smiled, finally understanding. This wasn’t a prison. It was a reward. He had been granted eternity in a world where nothing ever changed, and for the first time in his life, he felt perfectly, peacefully still.", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.6198107600212097}, {"token": " woke", "logprob": -0.060740288347005844}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.020681682974100113}, {"token": " the",